== 7. The Occasions of Sin

Now the seventh device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is by making the soul bold to venture on the occasions of sin. Satan says, “You may walk by the harlot’s door, though you will not go into the harlot’s bed. You may sit and dine with the drunkard, though you will not get drunk with the drunkard. You may look on #emph[Jezebel’s] beauty, and you may play and toy with #emph[Delilah,] though you do not commit wickedness with the one or the other. You may handle the golden wedge with #emph[Achan,] though you do not steal the golden wedge,” etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device are as follows.]

The first remedy is solemnly to dwell on those Scriptures that expressly command us to avoid the occasions of sin and the least appearance of evil. 1 Thess 5:22: “Abstain from all #footnote[Epiphanius says that under the old law, when any dead body was carried past a house, they were required to shut their doors and windows.] appearance of evil.” Whatever is heterodox, unsound and unsavory, shun it as you would shun a serpent in your way, or poison in your food. Theodosius tore up the Arians’ arguments, presented to him in writing, because he found them contrary to the Scriptures. And Augustine retracted even mere #emph[ironies,] because they had the appearance of lying. When God had commanded the Jews to abstain from #emph[swine’s] flesh, they would not so much as name it, but in their common talk would call a #emph[sow] by another name. To abstain from all appearance of evil is to do nothing in which sin appears, or which has a shadow of sin. Bernard glosses it finely:#footnote[Quicquid est male coloratum.] “Whatever has an evil look, or an evil report,” so that a man may wound neither his conscience nor his good name. We must shun, and be wary of, the very show and shadow of sin, if we care at all for our good name among others or our comfort at home. It was good counsel that Livia gave her husband#footnote[Socrates speaks of two young men who flung away their belts when, being in an idol’s temple, the purifying water fell on them, detesting, says the historian, the garment spotted by the flesh.] Augustus: “You must not only not do wrong, but not even seem to do so,” etc. So Jude 23: “And save others with fear, snatching them out of the fire, hating even the garment spotted by the flesh.” It is a phrase taken from legal uncleanness, which was contracted by touching the houses, the vessels and the garments of unclean persons. Under the law men might not touch a menstrual cloth, nor would God accept a spotted peace offering. So we must hate and avoid not only gross sins, but everything that may carry a savor or suspicion of sin. We must abhor the very signs and tokens of sin. So in Prov 5:8: “Keep your path far from her; do not go near the door of her house.” He who would not be burned must dread the fire; he who would not hear the bell must not meddle with the rope. To venture on the occasion of sin and then to pray, “Lead us not into#footnote[One said, “As often as I have been among vain men, I have come home less a man than I was before.”] temptation,” is the same as thrusting your finger into the fire and then praying that it may not be burned. So in Prov 4:14, 15 you have another command: “Do not set foot on the path of the wicked or walk in the way of evildoers. Avoid it; do not travel on it. Turn from it and pass on by.” This threefold progression of #emph[Solomon’s] shows with great emphasis how necessary it is for men to flee from all appearance of sin, as the seaman shuns sandbanks and shoals, and as men shun those who have plague sores running on them. As weeds endanger the grain, as infection endangers the blood, or as an infected house endangers the neighborhood, so the company of the bad endangers those who are good. Close intimacy with wicked companions is one of the strongest chains of hell, and binds us to a share both in sin and in punishment.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that ordinarily there is no conquest over sin unless the soul turns from the occasion of sin. It is impossible for the man who plays and sports with the occasions of sin to get the conquest of sin. God will not remove the temptation unless#footnote[The fable says that the butterfly asked the owl how she should deal with the fire, which had singed her wings, and the owl counseled her not to look so much as at its smoke.] we turn from the occasion. It is a just and righteous thing with God that the man who will venture to dance on the brink of the pit should fall into the pit, and that the man who will not flee from the occasions of sin should be a slave to sin. As long as there is fuel in our hearts for a temptation, we cannot be secure. He who has gunpowder about him had better keep far enough away from sparks. To rush on the occasions of sin is both to tempt ourselves and to tempt Satan to tempt our souls. It is very rare for any soul to play with the occasions of sin and not be ensnared by sin. It is seldom that God keeps from the acts of sin the soul that will not keep away from the occasions of sin. He who ventures on the occasions of sin is like the man who would quench the fire with oil, which is a fuel to maintain it and increase it. Ah, souls! Remember often how frequently you#super[+] have been overcome by sin when you have boldly gone on the occasions of sin. Look back, souls, to the days of your#super[+] folly, when you were conquered as easily as you were tempted, and vanquished as easily as you were assaulted, when you played with the occasions of sin. And therefore, as you#super[+] would be kept in the future from committing sin, and be made victorious over sin, oh, flee from the occasions of sin!

The third remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that other precious saints, who were once glorious on earth and are now triumphing in heaven, have turned from the occasions of sin as from hell itself. You#super[+] may see this in #emph[Joseph:] Gen 39:10: “And although she spoke to Joseph day after day, he refused to go to bed with her or even be with her.” Joseph was famous for all four cardinal virtues, if ever anyone was. In this one temptation you#super[+] may see his fortitude, justice, temperance#footnote[There are stories of heathens who would not look on women of excellent beauty, for fear of being ensnared.] and prudence, in that he shuns the occasion (for he would not so much as be with her). And what a man is in temptation, that he is indeed; for temptation is only a tap to let corruption run out. The #emph[Nazirite] was not only forbidden to drink wine; he might not even taste a grape, or the skin of a grape. The #emph[leper] was to shave his hair and pare his nails. The devil counts a fit occasion half a conquest. For he knows that corrupt nature has#footnote[Democritus plucked out his own eyes to avoid the danger of uncleanness.] a seedbed of all sin, which, once drawn out and watered by some sinful occasion, is soon set to work producing death and destruction. God will not remove the temptation until we remove the occasion. A bird is safe while she is aloft, but she never comes near the snare without danger. Shunning the occasions of sin makes a man most like the best of men. A soul eminently gracious dares not come near the powder train, though he is far from the blast. So Job 31:1: “I have made a covenant with my eyes.#footnote[ברית כרתי, I cut a covenant. In making covenants it was a custom among the Jews to cut some beast or other in pieces and to walk between the pieces, to signify that they desired God to destroy those who should break the covenant.] How then could I gaze with desire at a virgin?” I set a watch at the entrance of my senses, so that my soul might not be infected and endangered through them. The eye is the window of the soul, and if it were always open, the soul might smart for it. A man must not look intently at what he must not love entirely. The disciples were carried away by gazing at the beauty of the temple. It is best and safest to have the eye always fixed on the highest and noblest objects, as the mariner’s eye is fixed on the star while his hand is on the helm. So #emph[David,] when he was himself, shuns the occasion of sin: Ps 26:4, 5: “I do not sit with deceitful men, nor keep company with hypocrites. I hate the mob of evildoers, and refuse to sit with the wicked.” Stories speak of some who could not sleep when they thought of the trophies of other worthies who went before them. The highest and choicest examples are to some, and should be to all, very enlivening and stirring. And oh, that the examples of those worthy saints, #emph[David, Joseph] and #emph[Job,] might prevail with all your#super[+] souls to shun and avoid the occasions of sin! Everyone should strive to be like them in grace whom they desire to equal in glory. He who shoots at the sun, though he falls far short, will shoot higher than he who aims at a shrub. It is best (and it shows much of Christ within) to eye the highest and worthiest examples.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that avoiding the occasions of sin is an evidence of grace, and something that lifts a man above most other men in the world. What a man is in temptation, that he is indeed. And when sinful occasions present themselves before the soul, avoiding them shows both the truth#footnote[Plutarch says of Demosthenes that he was excellent at praising the worthy acts of his ancestors, but not so at imitating them. Oh, that this did not apply to many professing Christians in our times!] and the strength of grace. So it is when, with #emph[Lot,] a man can be chaste in #emph[Sodom;] and with #emph[Timothy] can live temperately in #emph[Asia,] among the luxurious #emph[Ephesians;] and with #emph[Job] can walk uprightly in the land of #emph[Uz,] where the people were profane in their lives and superstitious in their worship; and with #emph[Daniel] can be holy in #emph[Babylon;] and with #emph[Abraham] righteous in #emph[Chaldea;] and with #emph[Nehemiah] zealous in #emph[Damascus,] etc. Many a wicked man is swollen and full of sinful corruption, but does not show it for lack of occasion. But that man is surely good who will not be bad in his course, though tempted by occasions. A Christless soul is so far from refusing occasions when they come his way that he looks and longs for them. And rather than go without them, he will buy them, not only with love or money, but also with the loss of his soul. Nothing but grace can fence a man against the occasions of sin when he is strongly tempted to them. Therefore, as you#super[+] would cherish in your own bosoms a precious evidence of the truth and strength of your graces, shun all sinful occasions.
